    { label: "Role", value: "Developer, Technical Consultant" },
    {
      label: "With",
      value:
        '<a href="https://tewprai.com/IWillAlwaysThinkOfYouFondly" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="color: inherit;">Tewprai Bualoi</a>',
      html: true,
    },
    { label: "Exhibition", value: "EARLY YEARS PROJECT #4: PRAXIS MAKES PERFECT, Bangkok Art and Culture Centre (BACC)" },
    { label: "Revisited", value: "Speedy Grandma Centennial, 19 June – 31 July 2020" },
    { label: "Medium", value: "Research project, mixed media, 6 pieces" },
  ]} />

  <p class="mb-4">
    A research project in collaboration with Tewprai Bualoi exploring what forms a video file could
    inhabit. Together we manifested a digital object — a video file — into physical form without
    altering its original code, so that anyone who interacted with the piece could transform the
    physical object back into the original video file. That exploration of digital/physical
    alteration continues in Tewprai's practice since.
  </p>

  <p class="mb-4">
    My role was the programmer: building the tools that moved the file between states, and the
    application that could scan and reassemble it back into video.
  </p>

  <p class="mb-4">
    The project began when a dead laptop hard drive came back from data recovery on a brand-new
    replacement disk — the original rendered a carcass, its data intact elsewhere. That feeling of
    mourning a dead drive became the piece's starting point: the original HDD, dissected and
    presented as an anatomical study.
  </p>

  <p class="mb-4">
    The first experiment: opening a video file in plain text, saving all ~14 million characters as
    a .txt, then renaming the extension back to .mp4 — recovering the original file intact, as long
    as none of the text was touched. That became the project's method. We printed as much of it as
    we could — the longest paper available covered only one eighth of the total text.
  </p>

  <p class="mb-4">
    From there we looked for a way to make the physical form shorter without damaging the original
    file, testing materials that could outlast paper.
  </p>

  <figure class="my-8">
    <img
      src="/images/i-will-always-think-of-you-fondly/05-qr-codes.jpg"
      alt="132 printed QR codes arranged in a grid on a gallery wall"
      class="w-full"
      loading="lazy"
    />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-sm opacity-70">Untitled, 132 QR codes (2019)</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p class="mb-4">
    Finally, we encoded the file as a base64 string and split it into 132 QR codes — small enough to
    print, complete enough to scan back into the original video. I built the application that
    collects and joins each scanned code in sequence and reconstructs the file.
  </p>

  <p class="mb-4">
    The final piece pushes the material furthest: one QR code carved into marble, a medium that has
    carried inscriptions for thousands of years — the same file, given the site's oldest surviving
    format for permanence.
  </p>

  <p>
    First shown in EARLY YEARS PROJECT #4: PRAXIS MAKES PERFECT at BACC, the piece was revisited for
    <em>Speedy Grandma Centennial</em> (2020), presenting its full background — physical, published
    source code, and the originally-intended (but unrealized) online/virtual form — across physical,
    print, and web. Full documentation on
    <a href="https://tewprai.com/IWillAlwaysThinkOfYouFondly" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">tewprai.com</a>.
  </p>
</div>
